{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait`\n\n`Criterion::source_node_id` identifies the criterion's\n{term}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.source_node`:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Criterion<D: Domain>: Send + Sized {\n    // ...\n\n    /// The node this question is about.\n    fn source_node_id(&self) -> NodeId<D>;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait.source_node_id` has one obligation. `Criterion::source_node_id(&self) -> NodeId<D>` must return the criterion's source node, which is the node the question is about. The `references` tool shows the implementation is the trait declaration `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs#trait[Criterion]/fn[source_node_id]`.\n\n**Tests.**\n- `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[domain_persists_and_reuses_a_negative_validation_through_the_sdk]` calls the trait method directly on the production `Review` criterion. It asserts `review().source_node_id() == node`, where `node` is the requirement node the criterion was built for. It then stores and reloads evidence for that node and criterion path. A `load_evidence` for a different criterion path returns `None`.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested]` builds a `Review` whose `source` is `Node(1)`. Its `source_node_id` impl returns that stored source. After `verify`, the test asserts the resulting record's `source_node_id()` equals the interned `Node(1)`. After `store_evidence` and `load_evidence`, it asserts the loaded record's `source_node_id()` equals the verified record's.\n- `fn[wildcard_revision_does_not_supply_other_criteria_or_nodes]` in the same file changes `future.source` to `Node(2)`. It checks that evidence is not found under the other node. This shows the criterion's `source_node_id` drives record identity.\n\n**Gaps considered.**\n- A criterion that returned some node other than the one it was built for would be caught. The `sdk.rs` test compares the return value directly with the intended node. The `contracts.rs` test compares it with the interned `Node(1)`.\n- The requirement does not name other boundaries. The `contracts.rs` test uses only `Node(1)` for this assertion, but the `Node(2)` test in `fn[wildcard_revision_does_not_supply_other_criteria_or_nodes]` covers a second source node.\n\nI found no gap.",
